---
// Lesson 6.5 — Platforming Studio: The Gauntlet
// Build-day: one room, every jump skill, playtested to a rubric.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>6.5 — Platforming Studio: The Gauntlet</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Build day. Every skill from this unit — jump, ground check, feel
    mercies, double jump — goes into ONE room that a partner must
    survive blind. The grade is the room's clarity: a stranger should
    finish it with no instructions from you.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Studio Brief</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~40 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>The contract:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>One connected room (tilemap is fine, flat platforms are
            fine — polish is NOT the point)</li>
        <li><strong>Five sections</strong>, each testing one thing:
            (1) basic jump gap, (2) height — a jump UP two tiles,
            (3) a spike floor crossed by small platforms
            (<a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> spikes), (4) a
            <strong>soft gate</strong> — visible coin unreachable
            without the boots (<a href="6.4.html">6.4</a>), and
            (5) a turret on a ledge (<a href="3.1.html">3.1</a>)
            you pass UNDER, not over</li>
        <li>Start pad and end flag (a trigger that prints
            "GAUNTLET CLEAR" — full scenes come in Unit 7)</li>
        <li>Deaths must respawn at START (you know the loop)</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Rules of the studio:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>You may NOT explain the room verbally. Ever. The room
            teaches by layout.</li>
        <li>Every section must be passable two ways or with two
            timing windows — no pixel-perfect single solutions.</li>
        <li>Keep a notes file open: every "hmm" from a tester is a
            bug you have not coded yet.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Build order that works:</strong> floor and walls first
    → start flag → section by section, TEST EACH ALONE with a
    temporary teleport → then chain them → mercies LAST
    (<a href="6.3.html">6.3</a> numbers after the shapes are
    real). Teams that tune feel first end up tuning air.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Design bugs (they count)</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>A tester dies at your spike gap 4+ times in a row.
                That is not a hard jump — it is a READING bug. What
                did the room fail to say, and what geometry (not
                text!) fixes it?</li>
            <li>A tester grabs the boots and backtracks — past your
                soft gate to the coin. Wait: did they find the gate,
                or did they find NOTHING? What visual did you
                forget?</li>
            <li>A tester walks past the end trigger without
                noticing. Why is "print to console" not enough —
                and what is the cheapest visible response?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Silent-watching rules from <a href="2.4.html">2.4</a> apply,
        but today record NUMBERS per section (this habit becomes the
        dataset unit in <a href="9.5.html">9.5</a>):
    </p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Section</th><th>Deaths</th><th>Time (sec)</th><th>Tester said…</th></tr>
        <tr><td>1 — gap</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 — height</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 — spikes</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 — gate</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>5 — turret</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
    </table>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Iterate with numbers</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Any section with deaths ≥ 3 across two testers gets
                ONE change (not five). Which change did the notes
                point to — and why is "make it easier" almost never
                the right change?</li>
            <li>Swap rooms with another team. Play THEIR gauntlet,
                then say one sentence of admiration and one of
                cruelty. Both must be about geometry, not effort.</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"6.5a: gauntlet v1 + playtest notes"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Ship a five-section gauntlet that a stranger finishes blind</li>
            <li>Explain every section as a test of a specific unit skill</li>
            <li>Collect death/time data instead of vibes</li>
            <li>Make ONE numbered change per playtest round and re-test</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/index.html">Unit 6: Jumping</a> — everything this gauntlet tests</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/triggers-vs-collisions.html">Triggers vs Collisions</a> — the end-flag trigger</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.1.html">7.1 — Tilemaps: The Level Editor</a> — Unit 7 begins.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="6.5" title="6.5a Platforming Studio: The Gauntlet — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
